My approach was to start traversing the string from left to right and replace the first ‘6’ 
detected with ‘9’. 
Code: 
string solve(string num) { 
   for (int i = 0; i < num.length(); i++) { 
       if (num[i] == '6') { 
           num[i] = '9'; 
           break; 
       } 
   } 
   return num; 
} 

Question 2: A robot starts at the origin (0, 0) on a 2D X-Y plane. It can move one unit at a 
time in four directions: 
●​ ^ → North (up) 
●​ v → South (down) 
●​ > → East (right) 
●​ < → West (left) 
You are given a string ‘moves’, where each character represents one move made by the 
robot. 
Determine whether the path traced by the robot forms the boundary of a rectangle and 
returns to its starting position. 

4

Example 
Input: moves = "^>>>vvv<<<" 
Output: true 
Explanation:​
The robot moves around the perimeter of a rectangle and ends at the starting point. 
Code: 
bool solve(string &moves) { 
   unordered_map<char, pair<int, int>> dir = { 
           {'^', {-1, 0}}, 
           {'>', {0, 1}}, 
           {'v', {1, 0}}, 
           {'<', {0, -1}} 
   }; 
   vector<pair<int, int>> path; 
   int x = 0, y = 0; 
   for (char c : moves) { 
       x += dir[c].first; 
       y += dir[c].second; 
       path.push_back({x, y}); 
   } 


5

// Must return to the starting point 
   if (x != 0 || y != 0) 
       return false; 
   int minx = INT_MAX, maxx = INT_MIN; 
   int miny = INT_MAX, maxy = INT_MIN; 
   for (auto [cx, cy] : path) { 
       minx = min(minx, cx); 
       maxx = max(maxx, cx); 
       miny = min(miny, cy); 
       maxy = max(maxy, cy); 
   } 

   // Perimeter check 
   int perimeter = 2 * ((maxx - minx) + (maxy - miny)); 

   if (perimeter != (int)moves.size()) 
       return false; 

   // Every visited point must lie on rectangle boundary 
   for (auto [cx, cy] : path) { 
       bool onVerticalEdge = (cx == minx || cx == maxx); 

6

bool onHorizontalEdge = (cy == miny || cy == maxy); 
       if (!(onVerticalEdge || onHorizontalEdge)) 
           return false; 
   } 
   return true; 
} 

Question 3: You are given a three-digit integer N and an integer K. 
In one operation, you can increase any digit of N by 1 (a digit cannot exceed 9). You may 
perform at most K operations. 
Find the maximum possible three-digit number that can be obtained after performing at 
most K increments. 
Approach 
To maximize the number, prioritize increasing the most significant digit (hundreds place), 
then the tens place, and finally the units place. For each digit, use as many operations as 
possible (up to the amount needed to make it 9) before moving to the next digit. 
Code: 
int solve(int N, int K) { 
   string num = to_string(N); 
   for (int i = 0; i < 3 && K > 0; i++) { 
       int digit = num[i] - '0'; 
       int required = 9 - digit; 

7

int increase = min(required, K); 
       num[i] = char('0' + digit + increase); 
       K -= increase; 
   } 
   return stoi(num); 
} 

